geting the seat (Red to Blue, Trump super PAC counter-spending) keeps it competitive"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Steil: a slightly R-leaning district, incumbency, and a dominant fundraising edge, and most expert ratings land at Likely R while markets price him near two-in-one odds. A single Democratic-sponsored August poll showing a one-point race and heavy national attention on both sides argue for a closer-than-typical incumbent race, so the forecast sits a bit tighter than the rating consensus but still clearly Republican-leaning.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling and the scale of outside spending after the Red to Blue and super PAC investments could move this toward tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 46 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Bryan Steil (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Bryan Steil seeks a fourth term in a southeastern Wisconsin district that leans slightly Republican. He holds a decisive financial advantage, with roughly $6.3 million on hand compared with about $150,000 for Democrat Mitchell Berman, an ER nurse added to the DCCC's Red to Blue program. Most expert ratings call the race Likely Republican, though two outlets rate it a Tossup and markets give Steil about a two-in-three chance.\n\nThe race has drawn unusual national attention: a Trump-aligned super PAC is prioritizing it, and Democrats are campaigning hard on healthcare and ethics themes. A Democratic-sponsored August poll showed a one-point race, though it is the only recent public survey. The forecast expects Steil to win by a modest margin, with genuine but limited upside for Berman in a strong Democratic environment.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.3311, "margin": -2.62}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.3005, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3258, "margin": -3.5}]}